          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Yitzhak Rabin</strong><br>\nYitzhak Rabin was born in 1922 in Jerusalem, which was then part of the British Mandate of Palestine. His parents were immigrants from the third Aliyah, who arrived in Palestine from Eastern Europe. As a child, Rabin grew up understanding the complexities of Jewish-Arab relations and the Zionist movement.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Military Service</strong><br>\nRabin joined the Palmach, an elite unit of the Haganah, at the age of 18. He served with distinction during the Arab-Israeli War in 1948, which led to the independence of Israel. His military service played a significant role in shaping his later political career.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Political Rise</strong><br>\nAfter retiring from military service in 1967, Rabin entered politics. He served as ambassador to the United States, where he strengthened U.S.-Israeli relations. His diplomatic skills earned him a reputation as a pragmatic and effective leader.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
