          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Prime Minister of Israel</strong><br>\nYitzhak Rabin first became Prime Minister of Israel in 1974, succeeding Golda Meir. During his first term, he focused on strengthening the Israeli economy and military. He also began peace negotiations with Israel's neighbors.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Nobel Peace Prize</strong><br>\nRabin, along with Shimon Peres and Yasser Arafat, received the Nobel Peace Prize in 1994 for their work towards peace in the Middle East. The Oslo Accords, signed a year earlier, were a landmark in efforts to resolve the Israeli-Palestinian conflict.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Oslo Accords</strong><br>\nThe Oslo Accords represented a significant step toward peace, with Israel agreeing to Palestinian self-rule in parts of the West Bank and Gaza Strip. Rabin's support for the Accords showed his commitment to a peaceful resolution to the conflict.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
